		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=42393#p42393"><![CDATA[
Hi! Not sure you'll read this since your post is 1 month old, but anyway:<br><br>I'm working on a freeform robot building game that uses bullet physics too!  There was this robot game that had a following around 2013, but the devs abandoned it. A small group of players stayed regardless, and happily for us, the source code was retrieved this year. We're considering going open source.<br><br>Your game looks quite polished. Do you plan to have modding support? I don't know how compatible unreal engine is with modding, but an interesting thing about our fanbase is that the most dedicated players all have at least a modicum of programming ability. Our most prolific member modded the game via msil (already compiled c#) even back before we had source code access. I'm leading the development mostly because I'm the one with the most free time.<br><br>Your type of game attracts technical people. This helps for mods, just look how large kerbal space program's modding scene is.<br><br>Also, I think you have implemented networked physics for an online multiplayer mode? Thats really cool! Did you do that from scratch?<br><br>On one hand, part of me is disappointed that our amateur effort will likely be overshadowed now that a pro dev team is targeting the same niche. On the other hand,our fanbase has been hungry for a game like yours since a long time. I'm excited for them and wishes your team good luck.<br><br><br>The game is called rawbots and the discord is here discord.gg/HWzDMZc<br><br>Feel free to promote mainassembly on our discord<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=11365">z26</a> — Thu Sep 19, 2019 10:21 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
